# Sessions — {{PROJECT_NAME}}

Append-only ledger, newest first. One entry per session: what happened, and what
it produced. **Not read at startup** — it grows without limit by design, and
that is fine because nothing loads it. Startup reads `{{FINDINGS_FILE}}` instead.

Its job is to answer *"what happened, and when?"* months later — who changed
this, in which session, against which task. `{{FINDINGS_FILE}}` answers the
different question *"what do we know?"*. Keep them apart: a finding that is only
interesting as history belongs here, not there.

## Format

```
## YYYY-MM-DD · <session-id>
<Two to four sentences: what was attempted, what landed, what was abandoned and
why. Plain prose. State outcomes, not activity.>
Commits: [`<sha>`]({{FORGE_URL}}/commit/<sha>), [`<sha>`]({{FORGE_URL}}/commit/<sha>)
Branch: [`<name>`]({{FORGE_URL}}/src/branch/<name>)
Findings: <n> added, <n> withdrawn
Next: <the concrete next step, or "-">
```

- **session-id** — the Claude Code session identifier, so a transcript can be
  found again. Without it an entry is an anecdote. It is the filename stem of
  the transcript at `~/.claude/projects/<project-slug>/<session-id>.jsonl`.
  Write it **in full** — a truncated id reads better and cannot be pasted into
  a lookup, which is the only thing the field is for.
- **Commits** — every commit the session produced, linked. If a session produced
  none, say so explicitly; sessions that change nothing are worth knowing about.
- **Next** — what the following session should pick up. This is the handoff, and
  it is the field most often needed and least often written.

Write it even when the session went badly. A session that spent four hours ruling
an approach out is more useful to future-you than one that shipped something
obvious, and it is the one most likely to go unrecorded.

